{"task":"Allocate persite site and admin ports in step, so the second site starts","goals":"Fix cn-b3814414bca7: persite mode gave every site the same Caddy admin port, so any site after the first exited at startup with no warning","status":"Unreviewed","recorded_at":"2026-09-08T11:32:37Z","parent_commit":"9449766ac9a62554b2cbdcd0929971486fc2f4cd","repo":"ldev","pct":"100","tasks":[{"id":"alloc","content":"Allocate the (site, admin) pair together from sibling Caddyfiles, skipping bound ports","status":"completed"},{"id":"template","content":"Add the missing per-site Caddyfile template, with an absolute root","status":"completed"},{"id":"new","content":"Make ldev new write and validate the Caddyfile in persite mode","status":"completed"},{"id":"docs","content":"Write docs/persite.md, which install.sh already pointed at but never shipped","status":"completed"},{"id":"test","content":"Test allocation and end-to-end ldev new: 5 + 11 assertions","status":"completed"}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":[{"text":"Added `persite_used_ports` / `persite_next_ports` to `bin/ldev`: scans sibling `~/Sites/*.ldev/Caddyfile` files for claimed ports and returns the first (site, admin) pair free in both dimensions, walking 8443/2019 → 8444/2020 up to +200, with an optional live `nc -z` bind probe skippable via `LDEV_SKIP_PORT_PROBE=1`.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["architecture"]},{"text":"`cmd_new` now branches on MODE: auto mode keeps the old \"directory is the site\" behaviour; persite mode renders `templates/Caddyfile.persite.tmpl` into the new site dir with allocated ports, runs `caddy validate`, and prints the URL plus start command.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["architecture"]},{"text":"New `templates/Caddyfile.persite.tmpl`: per-site admin port, absolute `root`, `tls internal`, `php_fastcgi`, rolling access log, http→https redir, plus extensive comments on why the admin port must be unique.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["docs"]},{"text":"`install.sh` adds `SITE_PORT_BASE=8443` (persisted to the config file alongside `ADMIN_PORT`) and replaces the persite install blurb with the port-allocation explanation.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["build-config"]},{"text":"New `docs/persite.md` documenting per-site mode, the two-port scheme, the silent second-Caddy-exits failure, LaunchAgent `WorkingDirectory`/`ThrottleInterval` caveats, and an auto-vs-persite comparison.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["docs"]},{"text":"Two new test scripts: `test/persite-ports.test.sh` (unit-tests the allocator against a fake sites tree, including gap reuse and admin-only collisions) and `test/persite-new.test.sh` (end-to-end `ldev new` twice, asserting distinct port pairs, validation, no leftover placeholders, absolute root).","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["testing"]}],"code_quality":[{"text":"`bin/ldev` `persite_next_ports`: the loop body ends in bare AND-lists (`printf ... | grep -qx \"$site\" && continue`, and both `nc -z ... && continue`). When the grep/nc does *not* match, the AND-list's exit status is non-zero, so under `set -e` (which the rest of `bin/ldev` may enable) the function aborts the script instead of falling through to allocate the port — i.e. allocation dies exactly in the common case. The new tests deliberately run `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, so they cannot catch it. Verify the shebang/`set` line at the top of `bin/ldev`; if `-e` is on, rewrite each as `if ... ; then continue; fi`.","kind":"task","priority":"high","state":"complete","obsolete":true,"tags":["error-handling"],"history":[{"at":"2026-09-09T08:07:05.134Z","type":"refuted","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","note":"Marked obsolete."}]},{"text":"`test/persite-new.test.sh` and `test/persite-ports.test.sh` invoke `caddy validate` with no check that `caddy` is on PATH; on a machine without it the e2e test reports \"does not validate\" as a failure rather than skipping. Add a `command -v caddy >/dev/null || { echo \"  skip caddy validate\"; }` guard around the validation assertions.","kind":"task","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","effort":"quick","tags":["testing"]},{"text":"`persite_used_ports`'s `(admin localhost:|:)[0-9]{4,5}` matches every `host:port` in the file, so `php_fastcgi 127.0.0.1:9000` or a user-added `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000` also counts as \"used\". Harmless at the default bases (over-inclusive, never under-inclusive), but surprising if someone sets `SITE_PORT_BASE` near those.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["readability"]},{"text":"On validation failure `cmd_new` returns 1 but leaves the invalid Caddyfile on disk and has already printed \"created $dir\"; the site dir is half-built.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["error-handling"]}],"vulnerabilities":[{"text":"`bin/ldev` `cmd_new`: `$name` is taken straight from argv and interpolated into the `sed` replacement text (`-e \"s|__NAME__|$name|g\"`) and into the directory path with no validation. A name containing `|` breaks the sed expression, and `ldev new ../../foo` writes outside `$SITES` and outside the glob the allocator scans. This is a local dev CLI so the exposure is self-inflicted rather than remote, but a one-line guard (`[[ $name =~ ^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die`) removes both the injection and the traversal.","kind":"task","priority":"low","state":"complete","resolved_by":"6f7c292f47b586b514d079793c81c2f0623030f8","tags":["security"],"history":[{"at":"2026-09-09T08:09:16.105Z","type":"resolved","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","commit":"6f7c292f47b586b514d079793c81c2f0623030f8"}]},{"text":"No other security concerns: no secrets, no network fetches, no privilege escalation; the `nc -z` probe is loopback-only.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["security"]}],"code_style_suggestions":[{"text":"OK.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["readability"]}],"reusability":[{"text":"`test/persite-ports.test.sh` sources the allocator by `sed`-slicing `bin/ldev` between `^persite_*()` and `^}`; it silently extracts the wrong text if either function ever contains a column-0 `}`.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["testing"]}],"system_compatibility":[{"text":"`nc -z`, `grep -hoE`, `sort -un` and `seq` are fine on macOS/BSD (the stated target); `nc -z` with no `-G`/`-w` timeout can stall if 127.0.0.1 ever blackholes, though loopback makes that unlikely.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["compatibility"]}],"review_run":{"state":"ran","at":"2026-09-08T12:46:05Z","attempts":1,"agent":"claude","mode":"light"},"verification":{"checks":[{"id":"check-1","kind":"test","label":"test/persite-ports.test.sh — 5 assertions on pair allocation","status":"passed"},{"id":"check-2","kind":"test","label":"test/persite-new.test.sh — 11 assertions end-to-end; two sites validate and get distinct admin ports","status":"passed"}],"verified_at":"2026-09-08T11:42:47Z","verified_by":"claude-opus-5","ui_not_applicable":true,"ui_not_applicable_reason":"Shell CLI and a Caddy config template; output asserted with caddy validate."}}
